# Line endings, decided once, in the repository rather than per machine.
#
# ── Why this file appeared when the Android app did ────────────────────────
#
# For one project it did not matter: everything was authored on one laptop by
# one editor, so whatever it wrote is what got committed and nothing ever
# disagreed.
#
# Two projects and two toolchains is a different situation. `git subtree add`
# checked the Flutter files out fresh, Git for Windows applied its default
# CRLF conversion on the way, and every one of the 168 files came back reading
# as "modified" — 48,431 lines added and 48,431 removed, none of which were
# changes anybody made.
#
# It resolved itself, because the same setting converts back on comparison.
# That is the problem: it depends on a setting that is not in the repository,
# so it is correct on this machine and unknowable on any other. The first
# person to clone this with `core.autocrlf=false` would commit the whole tree
# back with different line endings and blame Dart.
#
# `text=auto` keeps every blob stored with LF — which is what they already
# are, so this changes nothing today — and lets each machine's checkout look
# however that machine prefers. It is the storage that has to be consistent,
# not the working copy.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts that must keep their native endings to run at all: a .ps1 or
# .bat with LF is usually fine, a .sh with CRLF is not, and the shebang line
# fails in a way that names the wrong thing.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
gradlew      text eol=lf
gradlew.bat  text eol=crlf

# Named explicitly rather than left to git's NUL-byte heuristic. It is a good
# heuristic, but a corrupted font or a mangled keystore is discovered late and
# diagnosed slowly, and the cost of being explicit is these nine lines.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.webp   binary
*.ico    binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.woff2  binary
*.jks    binary
*.keystore binary
*.stashit  binary

# Generated, and noisy in a review. Still committed — see the note on
# pubspec.lock in mobile/RELEASE.md — just collapsed by default on GitHub.
package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true
mobile/pubspec.lock linguist-generated=true

# So the repository's language bar reflects what is actually being written
# rather than what happens to have the most lines.
site/*.html linguist-documentation=true
